{
  "_comment": "esm-spec §9.5.5 load-time table checks and their pinned order. Each case is one function table (id `T`) and one `table_lookup` node referencing it. A harness builds the table IN MEMORY from `table` (writing each string \"NaN\" as a binary64 NaN — JSON cannot carry one, and several cases need it; the schema would also reject the one-value and empty axes, which is why these are not .esm documents), lowers the lookup through the binding's own §9.5.3 lowering, and asserts the diagnostic code (`expected_error_code`; null means the lookup lowers). Cases that break two rules break two ADJACENT rules of the order, so every adjacent pair is pinned; `breaks` lists the rules each case breaks, first the one to report. A binding whose table data cannot hold a NaN at all (its data type has no NaN value) may skip a case whose `data` holds \"NaN\", and says so in its harness.",
  "spec": "esm-spec §9.5.5 (Order of the table checks); formal: Esm.Closed.Tables.validateTable",
  "table_id": "T",
  "order": [
    "table_axis_duplicate_name",
    "table_outputs_duplicate_name",
    "table_interpolation_axes_mismatch",
    "table_axis_nan",
    "table_axis_non_monotonic",
    "table_axis_too_short",
    "table_outputs_length_mismatch",
    "table_data_shape_mismatch (nesting of data)",
    "table_data_nan",
    "table_data_shape_mismatch (shape assertion)"
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "control_valid",
      "description": "A well-formed 1-axis linear table. It lowers; no code. Proves the harness reaches the lowering with a table it built.",
